In JavaScript, data types are also divided into primitive and non-primitive (reference) types.

>> Primitive Data Types

> JavaScript has 7 primitive data types:

string
number
bigint
boolean
undefined
symbol
null

> Examples:

let name = "Alice";      // string
let age = 25;            // number
let isStudent = true;    // boolean
let score;               // undefined
let empty = null;        // null
let id = Symbol("id");   // symbol
let bigNum = 123n;       // bigint

> Characteristics:
Store a single value.
Immutable (their values cannot be changed directly).
Compared by value(Pass By Value)

> Example:

let a = 10;
let b = a;

b = 20;

console.log(a); // 10
console.log(b); // 20

Changing b does not affect a.

>> Non-Primitive (Reference) Data Types

Everything else in JavaScript is essentially an object.

> Examples:

Object
Array
Function
Date
Map
Set

-> let person = {
  name: "Alice",
  age: 25
};

-> let colors = ["red", "green", "blue"];

-> function greet() {
  console.log("Hello");
}

> Characteristics
Store collections of data.
Mutable (can be modified).
Compared by reference.

> Example:

let obj1 = { name: "John" };
let obj2 = obj1;

obj2.name = "Jane";

console.log(obj1.name); // Jane
console.log(obj2.name); // Jane

Both variables point to the same object in memory.

>> Primitive vs Non-Primitive in JavaScript
Primitive	                                                   Non-Primitive
string, number, boolean, null, undefined, symbol, bigint	   object, array, function, date, map, set, etc.
Stored by value	                                             Stored by reference
Immutable	                                                   Mutable
Compared by value	                                           Compared by reference

>> Example of comparison:

console.log(10 === 10); // true

console.log({} === {}); // false

The objects are different references, so they are not equal even though they look the same.
